1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,200
The Russian trek into space began

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:06,000
on the ground at Star City, the epicenter

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:09,740
of the Soviet space program, where an ordeal of training

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:12,640
prepared cosmonauts to survive the unknown dangers

5
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:13,640
of outer space.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Russia was enchanted with outer space long before spaceflight was real.

7
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:49,860
The word cosmonaut was first coined in a Russian silent movie,

8
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:54,140
where audiences thrilled to the exploits of a Slavic Buck Rogers.

9
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,980
But real spaceflight was anything but a fantasy.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,800
It was fraught with danger and uncertainty,

11
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:06,600
with no guarantee of a happy ending.

12
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:11,040
On the day of the first manned space launch in April 1961,

13
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,240
Soviet authorities prepared three press releases,

14
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:17,060
two in case the mission ended in disaster.

15
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:27,160
It was also conducted with the passion and power of war,

16
00:01:27,161 --> 00:01:31,060
A Cold War with the United States.

17
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:33,960
At the tip of the sword was the cosmonaut.

18
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:41,700
The boot camp and training ground where cosmonauts

19
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,740
prepared for space then and now was

20
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:49,120
Zyordny Gorodok, or Star City, the home of the Soviet space

21
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:51,820
program 40 miles northeast of Moscow.

22
00:01:51,821 --> 00:01:52,720
of Moscow.

23
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:59,000
They picked a spot right near a railway depot,

24
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:01,900
and they started building an apartment complex

25
00:02:01,901 --> 00:02:05,560
and an administrative building, and the first building

26
00:02:05,561 --> 00:02:07,380
to house any kind of training facilities.

27
00:02:07,381 --> 00:02:10,080
And they moved the cosmonauts out there.

28
00:02:10,081 --> 00:02:12,740
Their families would live in comfort,

29
00:02:12,741 --> 00:02:14,860
where their instructors would be around them

30
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:18,100
and part of their world on a day-to-day basis.

31
00:02:18,101 --> 00:02:20,340
Star City was a community dedicated

32
00:02:20,341 --> 00:02:24,280
to the exploration of space, a military and political priority

33
00:02:24,281 --> 00:02:29,040
of the Soviet government throughout the Cold War.

34
00:02:29,041 --> 00:02:31,380
Its approach to cosmonaut training

35
00:02:31,381 --> 00:02:34,680
embodied the essence of how the Soviets expected to beat

36
00:02:34,681 --> 00:02:37,120
Americans in space.

37
00:02:37,121 --> 00:02:39,600
Not only were the drills vastly different

38
00:02:39,601 --> 00:02:43,000
than the United States, so were the qualifications

39
00:02:43,001 --> 00:02:43,900
of the people picked.

40
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:49,700
The first several groups of American astronauts

41
00:02:49,701 --> 00:02:53,300
were selected out of the military test pilot pool.

42
00:02:53,301 --> 00:02:55,800
And they were selected because of their background,

43
00:02:55,801 --> 00:02:59,760
not just in flying unique machines or an aircraft

44
00:02:59,761 --> 00:03:02,700
the first time, but because they were trained in aeronautics

45
00:03:02,701 --> 00:03:05,100
so that they could communicate with the engineers

46
00:03:05,101 --> 00:03:07,340
and therefore improve the machines

47
00:03:07,341 --> 00:03:09,880
that they were actually testing.

48
00:03:09,881 --> 00:03:12,980
Six of the first seven were in their 30s.

49
00:03:12,981 --> 00:03:14,960
One was 40.

50
00:03:14,961 --> 00:03:18,700
On the other hand, the first cosmonauts, 20 in all,

51
00:03:18,701 --> 00:03:21,300
were new Soviet Air Force recruits.

52
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:24,640
They were young pilots sort of fresh off the dirt airfield

53
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:28,640
from about 24 to 26 years old with a couple hundred hours

54
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:30,940
of flying time, no higher education.

55
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:32,980
They had, in essence, been to a junior college that simply

56
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,980
trained them to fly.

57
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,280
While astronauts were expected to fly

58
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:42,060
and design their spacecraft, cosmonauts were not.

59
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:46,100
Sergei Korolev, the architect of the Soviet space program,

60
00:03:46,101 --> 00:03:50,100
intended for his cosmonauts to ride into space.

61
00:03:50,101 --> 00:03:54,380
Inside the capsule, the flight controls were locked.

62
00:03:54,381 --> 00:03:56,940
The only way a cosmonaut could get at them

63
00:03:56,941 --> 00:04:01,340
was if the ground control turned over a secret code.

64
00:04:01,341 --> 00:04:04,580
That event was reserved for the most dire of in-space

65
00:04:04,581 --> 00:04:06,060
emergencies.

66
00:04:06,061 --> 00:04:08,880
Otherwise, the spacecraft was controlled entirely

67
00:04:08,881 --> 00:04:11,220
from the ground under the ever-watchful eye

68
00:04:11,221 --> 00:04:13,720
of Korolev himself.

69
00:04:13,721 --> 00:04:15,900
He would have it as automated as possible.

70
00:04:15,901 --> 00:04:19,600
He knew it wouldn't require a very experienced pilot.

71
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:22,700
In fact, as he used to say, rabbits can fly my spacecraft.

72
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:26,740
The sole purpose of the cosmonaut was to survive.

73
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:29,640
They didn't need experience, just guts.

74
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:34,720
While Star City engineers worked out the technical details

75
00:04:34,721 --> 00:04:36,620
of rocketing a man into space,

76
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:40,000
its doctors instituted a regimen of training

77
00:04:40,001 --> 00:04:43,860
intended to make sure Soviet cosmonauts came back alive.

78
00:04:43,861 --> 00:04:48,400
But the problem was that space was a great unknown.

79
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:50,460
They were training for an environment

80
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:52,600
no one had ever been in.

81
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:56,300
There were questions, if not fears, about radiation,

82
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,600
weightlessness, the shock to the system of Lugdav,

83
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:04,520
or space sickness caused by severe disorientation.

84
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:08,620
Soviet planners identified 10 potential disaster scenarios

85
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:12,560
that could extinguish a cosmonaut's life in seconds.

86
00:05:12,561 --> 00:05:15,400
from a rocket failing to achieve orbital velocity

87
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:20,960
and falling back, to a crash landing during reentry.

88
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:24,300
As a result, doctors approached the training program

89
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,600
as if it were a lab experiment.

90
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,620
The poor cosmonauts were kind of rats in a maze,

91
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:33,520
and the people moving the cheese around

92
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:35,920
were the Soviet space doctors.

93
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:37,960
It was the doctors who were calling the shots

94
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:41,960
and the doctors who were running amok.

95
00:05:41,961 --> 00:05:44,960
Training was as much a preparation for space

96
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:47,860
as a test of a cosmonaut's physical, mental,

97
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:50,660
and even emotional limits.

98
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:54,900
They created a training that would basically stress

99
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,900
a human organism to the breaking point,

100
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,020
simply because they didn't know where that breaking point in space

101
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:02,520
might actually be.

102
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:07,520
The Soviets spent much more time on the survival aspects

103
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:11,220
and the escape systems, because the Americans

104
00:06:11,221 --> 00:06:13,020
were going to land in water.

105
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:14,820
They had different recovery systems

106
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:17,020
than the Soviets were designing to land on land.

107
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:19,820
And therefore, the programs evolved somewhat differently.

108
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:23,960
For example, the cosmonauts all learned to skydive.

109
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:26,560
Cosmonauts had to complete 40 jumps,

110
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:29,580
moving from static line jumping to free falls.

111
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:32,680
But skill wasn't the only training objective.

112
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:35,080
Another was enduring stress.

113
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:37,380
Cosmonauts had to recite poetry,

114
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:39,780
perform mathematical calculations,

115
00:06:39,781 --> 00:06:43,720
even jumped blindfolded to see if they cracked under pressure.

116
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:46,520
They had to prove that they were mentally strong enough

117
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:49,720
by skydiving and reacting in a few seconds to something

118
00:06:49,820 --> 00:06:53,420
that will take your life very quickly if you don't perform.

119
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:55,460
The unknowns of space comprised

120
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:58,640
a virtual encyclopedia of uncertainty.

121
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:01,140
The only hard data about orbital flight

122
00:07:01,240 --> 00:07:04,000
came from dogs launched into space.

123
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:05,580
One died in orbit.

124
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,640
Two others returned severely disoriented.

125
00:07:08,641 --> 00:07:11,840
Doctors theorized it was because there was no reference point

126
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:15,340
for up or down, so the ears' semicircular canals

127
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:18,480
did not register movement.

128
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:22,360
Doctors feared space would make cosmonauts too weak to fly,

129
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:25,160
so they trained them on extreme velocity swings,

130
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:28,360
high-speed sleds, and high-powered rotation machines

131
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:32,460
to toughen them up, or at least find out which cosmonaut was

132
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:36,460
best adept at keeping his head and stomach.

133
00:07:36,461 --> 00:07:39,800
There were a few kind of things that would swing people around

134
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:42,600
to test how quickly they got sick.

135
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:43,800
But it was pretty crude.

136
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,100
And the first cosmonauts quickly rebelled at it.

137
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:48,900
They just said, is this, we're not flying.

138
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:49,800
What are we doing here?

139
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:52,300
This is pointless.

140
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:53,920
Two candidates quit.

141
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:57,380
But the remaining 18 accepted the endless disorientation

142
00:07:57,480 --> 00:08:00,920
drills on fate as the only protection against the unknown.

143
00:08:00,921 --> 00:08:05,260
If nausea was one hurdle to overcome,

144
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:07,360
another was preparing a cosmonaut

145
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:09,660
to keep from going unconscious.

146
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:11,660
A key to building resistance to that

147
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:14,260
were workouts on the centrifuge machines.

148
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:20,580
Centrifuges are essentially a cockpit on the end of an arm,

149
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:24,580
and the arm just swings the cockpit, simulated cockpit,

150
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:28,220
in a circle, and it's forcing you to the outside

151
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:29,820
due to centripetal force.

152
00:08:29,821 --> 00:08:34,081
force and that simulates being in an aircraft that's pulling G's.

153
00:08:34,082 --> 00:08:35,720
It was also useful in the

154
00:08:35,721 --> 00:08:43,420
space program because the capsules were exposed to high acceleration during the launch and high

155
00:08:43,421 --> 00:08:50,370
deceleration during the re-entry. On the Earth's surface, humans are exposed to 1G.

156
00:08:50,371 --> 00:08:51,071
A person

157
00:08:51,072 --> 00:08:56,596
weighing 100 pounds exposed to 6G's feels as if they are 600 pounds.

158
00:08:56,597 --> 00:08:58,720
Under high G exposure, the

159
00:08:58,721 --> 00:09:03,560
The blood is actually pulled from the upper part of your body down towards your legs.

160
00:09:03,561 --> 00:09:07,800
If you can't keep the blood flowing from your heart to your brain, obviously you'll black

161
00:09:07,801 --> 00:09:10,920
out or pass out.

162
00:09:10,921 --> 00:09:15,820
This training in the centrifuge teaches you techniques to keep the blood flowing by tensing

163
00:09:15,821 --> 00:09:16,820
your body.

164
00:09:16,821 --> 00:09:23,720
That raises your blood pressure essentially and keeps the blood flowing to your brain.

165
00:09:23,721 --> 00:09:28,420
By the winter of 1960, the pressure became even more intense.

166
00:09:28,421 --> 00:09:32,320
Soviet Premier Khrushchev urged the space program to take

167
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:37,020
whatever steps necessary to beat the Americans into space.

168
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:39,660
In an all-out rush to get there first,

169
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:42,860
the training program suffered its first fatality.

170
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:45,700
It was a sobering reminder of the real dangers that

171
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:50,280
surrounded putting a human being into space.

172
00:09:50,380 --> 00:09:53,420
20 recruits entered the first cosmonaut training program

173
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:55,620
in March 1960.

174
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:58,180
It began with a seven-mile run through the woods

175
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:00,180
of Star City.

176
00:10:00,181 --> 00:10:05,580
At 9 a.m., they attended classroom lectures about weightlessness, g-force, and how a rocket

177
00:10:05,581 --> 00:10:06,780
worked.

178
00:10:06,781 --> 00:10:12,100
In the afternoon, they played hockey, an exercise in collegial teamwork.

179
00:10:12,101 --> 00:10:17,100
After 10 months of classroom lectures, college-like exams, and a battery of physical tests and

180
00:10:17,101 --> 00:10:21,360
challenges, the top six were elected to advanced training.

181
00:10:21,361 --> 00:10:26,000
Sergey Korolev, the head of the Soviet space program, took the opportunity to show the

182
00:10:26,001 --> 00:10:29,380
the men he called his young eagles, the Vostok capsule

183
00:10:29,381 --> 00:10:31,640
that would carry them into space.

184
00:10:31,641 --> 00:10:32,540
They were surprised.

185
00:10:32,541 --> 00:10:33,880
It was a cannonball.

186
00:10:33,881 --> 00:10:34,920
Where were the wings?

187
00:10:34,921 --> 00:10:37,460
You can imagine Soviet Air Force pilots kind of crowding

188
00:10:37,461 --> 00:10:38,240
around.

189
00:10:38,241 --> 00:10:41,420
You've got a couple of engineers in their white lab coats

190
00:10:41,421 --> 00:10:43,960
pointing out the various parts of the spacecraft.

191
00:10:43,961 --> 00:10:45,000
Here's the retro rocket.

192
00:10:45,001 --> 00:10:46,040
Here's the equipment module.

193
00:10:46,041 --> 00:10:49,840
Here's the straps that hold the sphere to the equipment

194
00:10:49,841 --> 00:10:51,240
module.

195
00:10:51,241 --> 00:10:58,120
Cosmonaut training put a high premium on intelligence and physical stamina.

196
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:04,140
But charm may have been equally important in advancing the career of a young cosmonaut named Yuri Gagarin

197
00:11:04,141 --> 00:11:07,040
when his boss asked him to sit in the space capsule.

198
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:10,720
Gagarin does something that apparently charmed Koryov.

199
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:16,380
Before he entered the spacecraft, he stopped and he knelt and he took off his shoes.

200
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:21,100
It was like the peasant entering the castle of the Lord of the Manor.

201
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:23,000
And Koryov was completely charmed by it,

202
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:26,320
because it showed that he was actually a fairly humble young

203
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:30,080
man, but also that he had a respect for what he was about

204
00:11:30,180 --> 00:11:34,460
to undergo.

205
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:38,220
The elite cosmonauts became known as the Vanguard Six.

206
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:41,760
They were the competition to America's Mercury 7 for the

207
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:46,400
prize of being the first in space.

208
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:49,600
Ironically, one of the key factors in the final selection

209
00:11:49,601 --> 00:11:51,000
was size.

210
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:54,040
A cosmonaut had to be around 5 feet 4 inches

211
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:57,240
and weighed no more than 160 pounds.

212
00:11:57,340 --> 00:12:00,820
The reason was that the Soviet engineers built a one size

213
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:02,620
fits all space suit.

214
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:04,780
As Korolev remarked, it was easier

215
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:07,460
to find the right size cosmonaut than to engineer

216
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:08,580
a different size suit.

217
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:15,600
By January 1961, cosmonaut training for the Vanguard 6

218
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:17,300
reached high gear.

219
00:12:17,301 --> 00:12:20,060
The Soviet launch vehicle, the Vostok, was ready.

220
00:12:20,580 --> 00:12:22,920
Though built to strike American cities in the Cold War,

221
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:25,580
it was set to carry the first man into space.

222
00:12:25,581 --> 00:12:30,080
It stood 126 feet tall, had four stages, 20 main engines,

223
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:35,600
and 12 smaller ones capable of reaching an orbital velocity of 18,000 miles in eight minutes.

224
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:38,900
Engineers had confidence in the Vostok.

225
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:44,600
The great unknown was whether or not a cosmonaut could survive riding it into space and back.

226
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:51,040
Ejection seats and launch sleds rehearsed parachute escapes.

227
00:12:51,140 --> 00:12:53,700
Disorientation capsules simulated a spacecraft

228
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:55,100
out of control.

229
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:59,040
And centrifuges surpassed the g-force of liftoff.

230
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:01,840
But the one condition trainers feared the most

231
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:04,060
was zero gravity.

232
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:07,260
They received opinion in aerospace medicine

233
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:09,660
was that you might go insane in weightlessness.

234
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:13,400
You might have some sort of bizarre physiological reaction.

235
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:15,360
No one quite knew for sure.

236
00:13:15,361 --> 00:13:19,200
Soviet test pilots flying aerial dives from 20,000 feet

237
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:22,000
accidentally encountered zero gravity.

238
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:24,300
It was applied to cosmonaut training

239
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:29,500
using Aleutian 76 aircraft, similar to the American KC-135s,

240
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:33,020
or the Vomit Comet, as it was called.

241
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:36,020
We can simulate being in a zero gravity environment

242
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:38,960
to get an aircraft that will do a parabola,

243
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:42,420
climb at 45 degrees, and then gently push over

244
00:13:42,421 --> 00:13:45,420
Gently push over so everything in the aircraft floats

245
00:13:45,421 --> 00:13:47,620
until it gets pointed downhill at 45 degrees

246
00:13:47,621 --> 00:13:48,660
and then pull out.

247
00:13:48,661 --> 00:13:51,900
That only enables you to have 25 or 30 seconds

248
00:13:51,901 --> 00:13:54,260
of zero gravity, and that's true zero gravity,

249
00:13:54,261 --> 00:13:56,600
but again, it's only a few seconds.

250
00:13:56,601 --> 00:13:58,980
But the weightless drill was nowhere long enough

251
00:13:58,981 --> 00:14:00,900
to duplicate the actual experience

252
00:14:00,901 --> 00:14:03,000
they would confront in space.

253
00:14:03,001 --> 00:14:04,900
The early cosmonauts were laboring

254
00:14:04,901 --> 00:14:09,020
under the pressure of knowing that this was largely

255
00:14:09,021 --> 00:14:12,520
the unknown, that everybody was sort of guessing,

256
00:14:12,521 --> 00:14:16,020
and they were going to be the ones who were on the front lines.

257
00:14:16,021 --> 00:14:18,220
Cosmonauts even trained for conditions

258
00:14:18,221 --> 00:14:21,460
that were statistically unlikely to occur,

259
00:14:21,461 --> 00:14:25,080
like being stranded on Earth.

260
00:14:25,081 --> 00:14:28,360
At best, Soviet doctors designed tests and trainings

261
00:14:28,361 --> 00:14:30,560
that prepared cosmonauts for space.

262
00:14:30,561 --> 00:14:32,520
But in many cases, training drills

263
00:14:32,521 --> 00:14:35,860
were also intended to see if cosmonauts could obey orders

264
00:14:35,861 --> 00:14:36,561
under duress.

265
00:14:39,020 --> 00:14:42,260
So they would put people in an isolation chamber

266
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:45,160
in the dark for days at a time, and just to see their reactions,

267
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:48,260
see how they followed orders, see how they continued

268
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:50,060
with the communications protocols.

269
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,660
They also did things like put them in heat chambers

270
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:55,100
just to sort of bake them for days at a time

271
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:59,380
to see whether living in a chamber that was 100 degrees

272
00:14:59,480 --> 00:15:02,380
for three or four days would drive them crazy.

273
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:04,820
The most dangerous isolation chamber

274
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:08,320
test of all put some cosmonauts in pure oxygen

275
00:15:08,321 --> 00:15:12,160
oxygen to see what breathing it might do to a body.

276
00:15:12,161 --> 00:15:14,460
A young lieutenant named Valentin Boderenko

277
00:15:14,461 --> 00:15:16,620
decided to cope with the week-long test

278
00:15:16,621 --> 00:15:19,720
by keeping a hot plate inside to cook meals.

279
00:15:19,721 --> 00:15:23,460
But that turned into a prescription for disaster.

280
00:15:23,461 --> 00:15:25,060
He had the medical leads attached

281
00:15:25,061 --> 00:15:28,320
and was taking them off and then cleaning the attach points

282
00:15:28,321 --> 00:15:31,980
on his skin with a little ball of cotton swabbed and alcohol.

283
00:15:31,981 --> 00:15:36,640
And he kind of carelessly flicked the swab onto a hot plate.

284
00:15:36,641 --> 00:15:39,740
the cotton ball ignited.

285
00:15:39,741 --> 00:15:42,520
The chamber ignited like a photo flashbulb

286
00:15:42,521 --> 00:15:45,140
and killed Boderenko.

287
00:15:45,141 --> 00:15:47,120
The timing couldn't have been worse.

288
00:15:47,121 --> 00:15:49,280
Launch day was fast approaching, and the time

289
00:15:49,281 --> 00:15:51,880
had come to make the final selection of the cosmonaut

290
00:15:51,881 --> 00:15:55,520
who would be first in space.

291
00:15:55,521 --> 00:15:58,560
Yuri Gagarin topped cosmonaut training's arduous drills

292
00:15:58,561 --> 00:16:01,240
with German Titov.

293
00:16:01,241 --> 00:16:04,200
But more than skill was required in the selection.

294
00:16:04,201 --> 00:16:06,580
As a contest in the Cold War, the government

295
00:16:06,581 --> 00:16:10,880
not just the best trained cosmonaut.

296
00:16:10,980 --> 00:16:14,520
Gagarin got the slight edge because he was, first of all,

297
00:16:14,620 --> 00:16:15,580
proletarian.

298
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:18,520
His parents came from a small farming village.

299
00:16:18,620 --> 00:16:20,660
His father was, in essence, a village carpenter,

300
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:23,960
whereas Titov's family was a little more bourgeois.

301
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:27,140
I mean, his father was a schoolteacher.

302
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:29,540
Gagarin was picked to be first in space,

303
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:31,540
but nearly lost the honor.

304
00:16:31,640 --> 00:16:35,180
The tragedy of Butarenko's death completely unnerved him.

305
00:16:35,181 --> 00:16:38,420
And General Nikolai Kamanan, the head of cosmonaut training,

306
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:41,560
nearly replaced him with German Titov.

307
00:16:41,660 --> 00:16:43,660
He got just a little nervous and apprehensive

308
00:16:43,760 --> 00:16:45,120
about all the other things.

309
00:16:45,220 --> 00:16:47,060
Are the parachutes working?

310
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:48,660
How is the ejection seat?

311
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:50,800
You know, where is my survival kit?

312
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:53,700
And Kamanan thought that showed he might be weakening a little

313
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:55,440
bit, not showing the right spirit,

314
00:16:55,540 --> 00:16:58,240
and was prepared to put Titov in his place.

315
00:16:58,340 --> 00:17:00,140
But then he looked back a little bit

316
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:03,980
and realized that it was justifiable.

317
00:17:03,981 --> 00:17:06,280
In spite of misgivings and apprehensions,

318
00:17:06,380 --> 00:17:08,780
after 18 months of intensive training,

319
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:12,020
27-year-old Gagarin was good to go.

320
00:17:12,120 --> 00:17:17,220
The day set for launch was April 12, 1961.

321
00:17:17,320 --> 00:17:21,200
He woke up on the morning of April 12, 1961,

322
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:25,240
and put his pressure suit on with its orange coverall

323
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:26,840
and the big fishbowl helmet.

324
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:30,640
And he marched out to the pad and gave a nice speech.

325
00:17:30,740 --> 00:17:32,940
He got in the spacecraft, and they lit it up,

326
00:17:32,941 --> 00:17:36,220
and they lit it up and the first words he said as it took off

327
00:17:36,221 --> 00:17:38,800
was the Russian word, ployakoli.

328
00:17:39,020 --> 00:17:42,300
And if you listen to him, it's as if he's saying, here we go.

329
00:17:51,420 --> 00:17:53,940
Gagarin endured six Gs at liftoff,

330
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:56,880
an orbital speed of 18,000 miles per hour

331
00:17:56,881 --> 00:18:00,000
and zero gravity 203 miles above the Earth.

332
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:04,940
Gagarin, to the ground.

333
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:09,440
During reentry, Gagarin's training

334
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:10,880
was put to the test.

335
00:18:10,980 --> 00:18:12,880
The craft started rotating wildly.

336
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:16,280
His vision became blurred under the force of 10 Gs.

337
00:18:16,380 --> 00:18:19,700
Finally, Soviet space managers, fearing a hard landing

338
00:18:19,800 --> 00:18:24,300
that might kill its pilot, had Gagarin eject from the capsule.

339
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:28,540
Air shooted out near Saratov and came down in a muddy field

340
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:31,860
where a peasant woman was standing there in horror

341
00:18:31,861 --> 00:18:35,360
in horror, and he's saying, no, no, I'm a Soviet man.

342
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:36,760
It's all right.

343
00:18:36,860 --> 00:18:38,660
Lead me to a telephone.

344
00:18:38,760 --> 00:18:41,100
Gagarin's flight, his first and last,

345
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:43,900
was over in 1 hour and 48 minutes.

346
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,440
He had achieved the one standard used to measure success.

347
00:18:47,540 --> 00:18:48,240
He survived.

348
00:18:51,340 --> 00:18:54,980
That same day, Gagarin flew to Moscow's Red Square.

349
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:58,180
The Soviet premier Nikita Khrushchev and thousands

350
00:18:58,280 --> 00:19:00,480
welcomed home the first man in space.

351
00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:09,640
While Gagarin was receiving a hero's welcome, Soviet doctors and scientists were analyzing

352
00:19:09,641 --> 00:19:13,402
every aspect of man's first space voyage.

353
00:19:13,403 --> 00:19:17,440
Much to their surprise, they discovered zero

354
00:19:17,441 --> 00:19:20,705
gravity didn't kill or even make Gagarin crazy.

355
00:19:20,706 --> 00:19:23,900
In fact, spaceflight was far less of an ordeal

356
00:19:23,901 --> 00:19:24,792
than training.

357
00:19:24,793 --> 00:19:29,760
With each mission of the Vanguard 6, the grip of doctors on cosmonaut training

358
00:19:29,761 --> 00:19:33,920
would loosen to a new approach to preparing cosmonauts for space.

359
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:41,640
Yuri Gagarin was more than the first man in space.

360
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:45,600
He was, in essence, the first human experiment in space.

361
00:19:46,380 --> 00:19:48,740
When he returned from his 108-minute flight,

362
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:53,040
he returned with the first data about the effects of space on the human body,

363
00:19:53,740 --> 00:19:56,400
opening the door for a generation of cosmonauts.

364
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:11,320
Gagarin proved that space orbit was possible, that humans could not only survive in space,

365
00:20:11,321 --> 00:20:13,000
but live in space.

366
00:20:13,001 --> 00:20:15,320
We used the path he paved.

367
00:20:15,321 --> 00:20:20,480
Gagarin proved that the training had been far more rigorous than it needed to be, and

368
00:20:20,481 --> 00:20:25,960
that you did not need to be a 25-year-old physical superman with a perfect heart rate

369
00:20:25,961 --> 00:20:30,460
and perfect blood pressure and the ability to withstand 12 Gs,

370
00:20:30,461 --> 00:20:33,160
to fly in space.

371
00:20:33,161 --> 00:20:35,460
More data was gathered four months later

372
00:20:35,461 --> 00:20:37,800
after a second cosmonaut, German Titov,

373
00:20:37,801 --> 00:20:40,600
launched in August 1961.

374
00:20:40,601 --> 00:20:43,480
If Gagarin showed rigorous training wasn't needed,

375
00:20:43,481 --> 00:20:45,640
Titov proved it didn't really work.

376
00:20:50,280 --> 00:20:54,020
German Titov was a full day in space.

377
00:20:54,021 --> 00:20:57,560
And unfortunately, Titov was sick for most of it.

378
00:20:57,660 --> 00:20:59,460
Titov, like future cosmonauts,

379
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:01,960
was struck with space motion sickness.

380
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:04,900
Scientists soon learned there was nothing like it on Earth,

381
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,900
nor was there any medication to treat or prevent it.

382
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,040
But later flights proved it went away in a matter of hours.

383
00:21:17,740 --> 00:21:19,740
With hard information, the Soviets

384
00:21:19,840 --> 00:21:22,780
began to reshape the cosmonaut training program,

385
00:21:22,781 --> 00:21:25,580
beginning with a new choice of candidates.

386
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:28,720
They no longer searched for young, super-fit humans.

387
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:32,620
They now sought older candidates who, like American astronauts,

388
00:21:32,720 --> 00:21:37,420
had engineering degrees and Air Force Command experience.

389
00:21:37,520 --> 00:21:41,140
They also chose women.

390
00:21:41,240 --> 00:21:43,940
Among the first was Valentina Tereshkova,

391
00:21:44,040 --> 00:21:47,080
a young textile worker, parachute hobbyist,

392
00:21:47,180 --> 00:21:52,780
and now Star City's first female cosmonaut.

393
00:21:52,781 --> 00:21:55,820
The rigorous training that had applied to the first cosmonauts

394
00:21:55,821 --> 00:21:57,280
really didn't apply here.

395
00:21:57,281 --> 00:22:00,920
They were subjected to far fewer tests.

396
00:22:00,921 --> 00:22:04,560
There was going to be a greatly reduced number of chamber tests

397
00:22:04,561 --> 00:22:08,300
and observations and medical examinations.

398
00:22:08,301 --> 00:22:10,540
The classroom training was shortened somewhat

399
00:22:10,541 --> 00:22:13,840
because they, again, had a better idea of what a cosmonaut

400
00:22:13,841 --> 00:22:16,800
needed to know to survive a flight.

401
00:22:16,801 --> 00:22:20,840
Tereshkova launched in 1963.

402
00:22:20,841 --> 00:22:26,560
The widely held belief that space required ordeal-like tests of endurance evaporated

403
00:22:26,561 --> 00:22:32,120
as a confident Soviet space program showed the world that a woman could ride into space.

404
00:22:38,200 --> 00:22:42,040
Tereshkova's flight marked an end to the regimen of cosmonaut training

405
00:22:42,041 --> 00:22:46,160
that stressed physical conditioning and marked the beginning of a new approach

406
00:22:46,161 --> 00:22:48,960
that stressed the use of simulators and mock-ups.

407
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:56,980
The Soviets now aim for the moon and longer flights in space.

408
00:22:58,340 --> 00:23:01,580
In order to be trained for these much more complex missions

409
00:23:01,581 --> 00:23:05,900
that would involve maneuvering in space and long-duration flights

410
00:23:05,901 --> 00:23:08,660
and rendezvous and docking with other spacecraft,

411
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:11,640
obviously the training facilities had to be upgraded.

412
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:14,120
The first thing that happened is that the Soviet Air Force

413
00:23:14,121 --> 00:23:16,920
took the Cosmonaut Training Center, took Star City,

414
00:23:16,921 --> 00:23:19,280
away from the aerospace medical people

415
00:23:19,281 --> 00:23:22,680
and made it, in essence, a different kind of military unit.

416
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:29,640
They weren't just parachutists who were kind of being shot out of a cannon to survive a riot around the world now,

417
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:31,660
just to impress the world and impress the Americans.

418
00:23:32,140 --> 00:23:37,760
Now they were supposed to be part of crews building spacecraft for the exploitation of space.

419
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:45,760
Star City still resorted to arduous physical training with isolation chambers, centrifuges, and high-velocity swings.

420
00:23:45,761 --> 00:23:48,600
But now that weightlessness and space sickness were not seen

421
00:23:48,700 --> 00:23:52,200
as dangerous, cosmonauts evolved from passengers to pilots

422
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:54,200
and now required more skill training.

423
00:23:57,100 --> 00:24:00,000
Ironically, one of its prime sources of information

424
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:02,420
as it reinvented cosmonaut training

425
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:06,620
came from its space rival, the United States.

426
00:24:06,720 --> 00:24:08,620
We have you go for orbit here.

427
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:09,720
Go for orbit.

428
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:15,060
NASA was substantially far ahead

429
00:24:15,061 --> 00:24:20,060
They had done a whole variety of things the Soviets were having a tough time doing.

430
00:24:20,061 --> 00:24:26,060
The training methods, the simulators that NASA had, all of which were public knowledge.

431
00:24:26,061 --> 00:24:35,684
You could write to NASA and get handy pictures and probably diagrams of all of them, and adapt it to their purposes so that they would hopefully have more capable

432
00:24:35,685 --> 00:24:38,060
space crews and more effective training.

433
00:24:38,061 --> 00:24:43,060
Star City boosted cosmonaut training to a new level of technical proficiency.

434
00:24:43,061 --> 00:24:47,060
But the old problem of training for the unknown still remained.

435
00:24:47,160 --> 00:24:50,300
Near disaster and death would penetrate the vulnerabilities

436
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:53,540
of a program charged with training people on Earth

437
00:24:53,640 --> 00:24:55,600
for a mission in outer space.

438
00:25:05,780 --> 00:25:08,720
By 1964, the Soviet space program

439
00:25:08,820 --> 00:25:10,920
was devoted to landing on the moon,

440
00:25:10,921 --> 00:25:16,760
the moon, a new phase of competition with the United States. Central to that success

441
00:25:16,761 --> 00:25:22,032
was training cosmonauts to leave a space capsule and walk in space.

442
00:25:22,033 --> 00:25:23,920
To accomplish that feat,

443
00:25:23,921 --> 00:25:29,260
engineers developed a special pressurized suit, then made its testing a year-long part

444
00:25:29,261 --> 00:25:30,692
of cosmonaut training.

445
00:25:30,693 --> 00:25:35,380
When it was over, cosmonaut Alexei Leonov was selected to take the first

446
00:25:35,381 --> 00:25:38,620
spacewalk in March 1965.

447
00:25:38,621 --> 00:25:40,980
They tried to prepare him for the experience

448
00:25:40,981 --> 00:25:43,660
by putting him through all kinds of rigorous physical training,

449
00:25:43,661 --> 00:25:46,400
by having him learn how to swim and dive,

450
00:25:46,401 --> 00:25:49,060
by putting him in the zero-g aircraft,

451
00:25:49,061 --> 00:25:51,460
getting it out of the suit, by putting him in the chamber

452
00:25:51,461 --> 00:25:53,700
and bleeding the air out of it.

453
00:25:53,701 --> 00:25:56,700
Still, the only way to test the pressurized suit

454
00:25:56,701 --> 00:26:00,600
and the viability of a spacewalk was to do it.

455
00:26:00,601 --> 00:26:03,880
Nothing on Earth prepared him for what was about to happen

456
00:26:03,881 --> 00:26:07,420
as the spacesuit began to react differently than it had on the ground.

457
00:26:08,300 --> 00:26:12,680
The spacesuit became rigid like a balloon,

458
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:15,020
far more than anyone had anticipated on Earth,

459
00:26:15,220 --> 00:26:19,740
the combination of lack of air pressure and lack of gravity.

460
00:26:19,900 --> 00:26:22,480
In fact, it ballooned so thoroughly

461
00:26:22,481 --> 00:26:25,840
that his fingers were out of the gloves at one point.

462
00:26:25,841 --> 00:26:30,500
It just expanded beyond the ability of his hands to remain in the gloves.

463
00:26:30,501 --> 00:26:34,500
The cosmonaut could not fit back through the airlock.

464
00:26:34,501 --> 00:26:38,500
Leonov dropped the pressure inside the suit by almost 200%

465
00:26:38,501 --> 00:26:44,500
by opening a special seal, a quick improvisation that saved his life.

466
00:26:44,501 --> 00:26:50,500
Leonov survived, but his near disaster transformed cosmonaut training at Star City.

467
00:26:50,501 --> 00:26:55,500
The new directive was to manufacture better, more realistic space-like environments

468
00:26:55,501 --> 00:26:59,200
space-like environments and immerse cosmonauts into them

469
00:26:59,201 --> 00:27:02,500
to rehearse space missions.

470
00:27:02,501 --> 00:27:06,100
To do that, cosmonauts began training in water.

471
00:27:06,101 --> 00:27:11,900
It was impossible to recreate the space environment on Earth.

472
00:27:11,901 --> 00:27:14,400
There were compromises you could make.

473
00:27:14,401 --> 00:27:17,800
Having people simulate spacewalks in underwater tanks,

474
00:27:17,801 --> 00:27:23,560
that became a common practice after the mid-1960s.

475
00:27:23,561 --> 00:27:27,640
Spacewalks were practiced in a new training pool, or neutral buoyancy tank.

476
00:27:28,300 --> 00:27:32,000
It held 1.3 million gallons of water, 36 feet deep.

477
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:37,680
In spacesuits bound with weights and pumped full of air,

478
00:27:38,260 --> 00:27:43,020
cosmonauts practice maneuvers, from ratcheting bolts to entering and exiting a capsule.

479
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:45,020
It is still used today.

480
00:27:46,420 --> 00:27:50,560
The reason the pool provides us such good training for people in a spacesuit

481
00:27:50,561 --> 00:27:56,400
is you can take a 150-pound person, put a 100-pound space suit on them, put them in the pool,

482
00:27:56,540 --> 00:28:00,780
and they can function almost like they're in a weightless environment

483
00:28:00,781 --> 00:28:06,900
because we have assistant divers that will attach weights at different locations on that suit

484
00:28:06,901 --> 00:28:14,080
so that they're buoyant, so they don't have to hang on to a handle or stand on a platform.

485
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:18,120
And what that enables them to do in the pool is if they're using a tool,

486
00:28:18,121 --> 00:28:21,600
their body will react much as if it's in zero gravity.

487
00:28:21,601 --> 00:28:23,460
We just have to be in an environment that makes you

488
00:28:23,461 --> 00:28:27,300
think about force reactions when you exert yourself

489
00:28:27,301 --> 00:28:29,460
or move a mass.

490
00:28:29,461 --> 00:28:31,660
Besides the new neutral buoyancy tanks,

491
00:28:31,661 --> 00:28:34,360
cosmonaut training began using flight simulators

492
00:28:34,361 --> 00:28:35,840
and spacecraft mock-ups.

493
00:28:40,980 --> 00:28:44,340
In our mission, we learned what to expect from the simulators.

494
00:28:44,341 --> 00:28:46,560
Physical conditioning is always important.

495
00:28:46,561 --> 00:28:50,560
The body has to be strong and in shape or else it's impossible to be in space.

496
00:28:51,760 --> 00:28:54,560
But simulators add a new degree of confidence.

497
00:28:56,560 --> 00:29:03,560
Being able to put crews in those simulators is the single biggest innovation in spaceflight training

498
00:29:03,561 --> 00:29:08,560
that both sides came to and turned out to be the thing that in essence made all the difference.

499
00:29:08,561 --> 00:29:12,560
They would mimic the operations of a real spacecraft.

500
00:29:12,561 --> 00:29:17,680
If you threw a switch that turned on the lights and lowered the air pressure, the same thing

501
00:29:17,681 --> 00:29:18,760
would happen in the spacecraft.

502
00:29:18,761 --> 00:29:24,960
And this allowed them to put crew members together to rehearse every moment of every

503
00:29:24,961 --> 00:29:30,080
spaceflight, and not just spaceflights as they went well, but more importantly, to rehearse

504
00:29:30,081 --> 00:29:31,520
the days when things don't go well.

505
00:29:32,780 --> 00:29:37,720
Simulators and mock-ups improved cosmonaut training, but it didn't win the race to the

506
00:29:37,721 --> 00:29:38,421
moon.

507
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:40,520
Superior rocket technology did.

508
00:29:42,560 --> 00:29:44,060
It's raining, John.

509
00:29:44,061 --> 00:29:45,800
Look at it go, would you, Charlie?

510
00:29:45,801 --> 00:29:47,100
You got all your steering.

511
00:29:47,101 --> 00:29:48,700
It's great.

512
00:29:48,701 --> 00:29:52,940
After the Americans landed on a lunar surface in 1969,

513
00:29:52,941 --> 00:29:55,740
the Soviets refocused on a new objective,

514
00:29:55,741 --> 00:29:57,200
long-term space flight.

515
00:29:57,201 --> 00:29:58,380
There you go.

516
00:29:58,381 --> 00:30:00,080
Beautiful, baby.

517
00:30:00,081 --> 00:30:03,140
Cosmonauts prepared for expedition length space flight

518
00:30:03,141 --> 00:30:05,140
with rigorous ground training.

519
00:30:05,141 --> 00:30:09,320
In June 1970, cosmonauts Nikolaev and Sevastanov

520
00:30:09,321 --> 00:30:14,220
went into space for a record 18 days.

521
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:18,760
They returned back absolutely devastating,

522
00:30:18,860 --> 00:30:20,160
you see, I might say.

523
00:30:20,260 --> 00:30:22,460
After that, we decided that the space station,

524
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:26,080
it's maybe kind of a path for the future in the main space

525
00:30:26,180 --> 00:30:28,540
flight with a training facility on board,

526
00:30:28,640 --> 00:30:35,440
with a big space for a human to be stationed in space.

527
00:30:35,540 --> 00:30:38,320
The prolonged weightlessness of 18 days in space

528
00:30:38,321 --> 00:30:40,860
resulted in many problems.

529
00:30:40,861 --> 00:30:43,000
The skeletal and muscle systems were weakened

530
00:30:43,001 --> 00:30:45,020
by as much as 40%.

531
00:30:45,021 --> 00:30:49,600
The cosmonauts lost so much water, they were dehydrated.

532
00:30:49,601 --> 00:30:54,040
There was even a decrease in leg size.

533
00:30:54,041 --> 00:30:55,940
Soviet planners realized they needed

534
00:30:55,941 --> 00:30:58,380
to train while in space.

535
00:30:58,381 --> 00:31:02,520
And they needed a bigger craft to do that, a space station.

536
00:31:02,521 --> 00:31:05,740
The vehicle they developed was the Salyut, a three-chamber

537
00:31:05,741 --> 00:31:09,340
space station, the first ever built.

538
00:31:09,341 --> 00:31:16,000
No two missions are alike, but the most dangerous are always the first ones where new maneuvers

539
00:31:16,001 --> 00:31:21,380
are tested, like the first man in space or the first spacewalk, and unfortunately, the

540
00:31:21,381 --> 00:31:23,780
first mission to our space station.

541
00:31:23,781 --> 00:31:29,980
You can only know what to expect after it's over, and then sometimes it's too late.

542
00:31:29,981 --> 00:31:35,440
Cosmonaut training was like a suit of armor intended to shield cosmonauts from space dangers.

543
00:31:35,441 --> 00:31:39,200
No matter how thick the armor, there were always areas of vulnerability.

544
00:31:40,080 --> 00:31:42,860
That was especially true of training for the space station.

545
00:31:45,860 --> 00:31:53,080
The idea of putting crews in that kind of environment forced a radical change in the way training was conducted.

546
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:57,460
You had to spend much more time training them in how to physically live in space.

547
00:31:57,461 --> 00:32:03,780
It wasn't just let's eat food out of the squeeze tubes and things like that and remain strapped in.

548
00:32:03,781 --> 00:32:05,780
And they had to learn how to exercise.

549
00:32:05,781 --> 00:32:07,780
They had to learn how to manage their diet.

550
00:32:07,781 --> 00:32:14,780
There was a whole variety of psychological and physiological work that needed to be done.

551
00:32:14,781 --> 00:32:20,780
The first mission ever undertaken to a space station turned out to be disastrous.

552
00:32:20,781 --> 00:32:28,780
In June 1971, cosmonauts Volkov, Vyborovsky, and Patsayev, three senior Air Force pilots,

553
00:32:28,781 --> 00:32:31,780
crewed the mission to the Salyut space station.

554
00:32:31,781 --> 00:32:33,780
was to last for one month.

555
00:32:33,781 --> 00:32:37,780
Ironically, they were not the primary cosmonauts,

556
00:32:37,781 --> 00:32:38,780
but a backup team.

557
00:32:38,781 --> 00:32:42,780
They had trained individually, but not that long as a team,

558
00:32:42,781 --> 00:32:45,780
and were cleared for takeoff after a primary crew member

559
00:32:45,781 --> 00:32:48,780
became ill days before liftoff.

560
00:32:48,781 --> 00:32:52,780
Typically, the capsule could only accommodate two cosmonauts,

561
00:32:52,781 --> 00:32:54,780
but the mission required three.

562
00:32:54,781 --> 00:32:56,780
To fit the extra man, the decision was made

563
00:32:56,781 --> 00:32:59,780
to abandon their pressurized spacesuits,

564
00:32:59,781 --> 00:33:03,020
a deadly idea if the capsule were to lose pressure.

565
00:33:04,140 --> 00:33:05,600
The launch was perfect.

566
00:33:05,880 --> 00:33:08,660
Three days later, the capsule docked with the space station,

567
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:11,000
a maneuver practiced in simulator training.

568
00:33:11,880 --> 00:33:13,460
But the mission was troubled.

569
00:33:13,820 --> 00:33:17,260
On board, a small fire broke up, equipment broke down,

570
00:33:17,480 --> 00:33:18,800
and tempers flared up.

571
00:33:19,160 --> 00:33:21,520
Ground control ordered the crew back a week early.

572
00:33:21,521 --> 00:33:25,160
The crew sort of staggered through its mission,

573
00:33:25,880 --> 00:33:28,040
and equipment did keep failing.

574
00:33:28,041 --> 00:33:31,440
There were tremendous personal conflicts within the crew.

575
00:33:31,540 --> 00:33:33,440
In spite of the problems, the crew

576
00:33:33,540 --> 00:33:36,340
completed its mission, becoming the first to rendezvous,

577
00:33:36,440 --> 00:33:39,780
dock, and stay aboard a space station.

578
00:33:39,880 --> 00:33:42,480
But during reentry, trouble began.

579
00:33:42,580 --> 00:33:45,400
Ground controllers lost contact with the crew, perhaps

580
00:33:45,500 --> 00:33:47,200
because of a broken antenna.

581
00:33:47,300 --> 00:33:50,100
Nonetheless, the capsule returned safely.

582
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:52,600
But the recovery team soon discovered the reason

583
00:33:52,700 --> 00:33:54,540
for the communications breakdown.

584
00:33:54,640 --> 00:33:58,040
When they opened the capsule, they found the crew dead.

585
00:33:58,041 --> 00:34:02,040
They had died of embolisms.

586
00:34:02,041 --> 00:34:06,040
There was a valve on their Soyuz orbital module

587
00:34:06,041 --> 00:34:08,040
that opened prematurely.

588
00:34:08,041 --> 00:34:10,040
It opened while they were still in space,

589
00:34:10,041 --> 00:34:12,040
after they had discarded their reentry module,

590
00:34:12,041 --> 00:34:16,040
and the air started whistling out of the spacecraft.

591
00:34:16,041 --> 00:34:18,040
The spacecraft depressurized and the crew died

592
00:34:18,041 --> 00:34:20,040
within probably 30 seconds.

593
00:34:20,041 --> 00:34:22,040
They were unable to find the valve,

594
00:34:22,041 --> 00:34:25,040
find the handle to crank to close that valve

595
00:34:25,041 --> 00:34:28,436
closed that valve because it was kind of hidden beneath one of the seats.

596
00:34:28,437 --> 00:34:29,460
It was not supposed to

597
00:34:29,461 --> 00:34:31,460
be operated at this time.

598
00:34:31,461 --> 00:34:37,300
After the deaths of the three cosmonauts, Star City took a harder look at

599
00:34:37,301 --> 00:34:38,463
cosmonaut training.

600
00:34:38,464 --> 00:34:43,420
Individual training continued, but new drills were instituted that focused on how

601
00:34:43,421 --> 00:34:44,851
crews worked together.

602
00:34:44,852 --> 00:34:49,600
The exercises were conducted on water and land to simulate life and death

603
00:34:49,601 --> 00:34:51,160
emergencies.

604
00:34:51,161 --> 00:34:55,060
A favorite proving ground was the wilds of Siberia in winter.

605
00:34:55,061 --> 00:34:57,440
Crews had to spend up to three days and two nights

606
00:34:57,441 --> 00:34:59,820
on the coast of the Arctic Sea, where temperatures

607
00:34:59,821 --> 00:35:03,100
fell to 40 degrees below zero and minus 60

608
00:35:03,101 --> 00:35:04,520
with the wind chill.

609
00:35:04,521 --> 00:35:07,180
The crews were observed to see how they got along,

610
00:35:07,181 --> 00:35:09,160
who gave orders, and who followed them

611
00:35:09,161 --> 00:35:12,420
in the course of constructing shelter, building fires,

612
00:35:12,421 --> 00:35:13,960
and preparing meals.

613
00:35:13,961 --> 00:35:17,420
How cosmonaut crews worked together under extreme duress

614
00:35:17,421 --> 00:35:20,260
became a key ingredient in picking crews.

615
00:35:20,261 --> 00:35:22,280
The Soviet trainers vow that the deaths

616
00:35:22,281 --> 00:35:24,260
of the first space station cosmonauts

617
00:35:24,261 --> 00:35:27,080
would not be in vain was realized.

618
00:35:27,081 --> 00:35:30,060
No cosmonaut ever died in space again.

619
00:35:31,320 --> 00:35:33,620
But a greater threat faced Star City,

620
00:35:33,621 --> 00:35:36,860
one that even cosmonaut training could not guard against.

621
00:35:36,861 --> 00:35:39,560
28 years after cosmonaut training began,

622
00:35:39,561 --> 00:35:41,700
the Soviet Union fell.

623
00:35:41,701 --> 00:35:43,220
In its cataclysmic crash,

624
00:35:43,221 --> 00:35:45,920
Star City and the Soviet space program

625
00:35:45,921 --> 00:35:49,280
were thrown into chaos.

626
00:35:49,281 --> 00:35:52,940
Star City, the home of the Soviet and Russian space program,

627
00:35:52,941 --> 00:35:55,800
kept pace with the training needs of every new cosmonaut

628
00:35:55,801 --> 00:35:58,700
mission, moving from conditioning on the ground

629
00:35:58,701 --> 00:36:00,820
to conditioning in flight.

630
00:36:00,821 --> 00:36:04,180
The duress of zero gravity during expedition-length flights

631
00:36:04,181 --> 00:36:07,260
required cosmonauts to train aboard the space stations

632
00:36:07,261 --> 00:36:10,240
to maintain skeletal and muscle strength.

633
00:36:10,241 --> 00:36:13,360
While cosmonaut training literally reached new heights,

634
00:36:13,361 --> 00:36:16,400
big changes were underway on the ground.

635
00:36:16,401 --> 00:36:19,200
The one secret Soviet home of space exploration

636
00:36:19,201 --> 00:36:22,300
did something that was previously inconceivable.

637
00:36:22,301 --> 00:36:26,440
It invited American astronauts to its training program.

638
00:36:26,441 --> 00:36:30,220
In 1972, the United States and Soviet Union

639
00:36:30,221 --> 00:36:33,520
signed a treaty to begin working on a joint project known

640
00:36:33,521 --> 00:36:35,580
as the Apollo-Soyez mission.

641
00:36:35,581 --> 00:36:41,020
For the first time, Americans came to Star City.

642
00:36:41,021 --> 00:36:43,300
That presented a giant problem that was a little secret

643
00:36:43,301 --> 00:36:47,300
So they'd actually built a hotel for the American astronauts.

644
00:36:47,400 --> 00:36:49,940
And at the same time, realizing that Apollo Soyuz

645
00:36:50,040 --> 00:36:52,340
was only the one flight, they started looking around

646
00:36:52,440 --> 00:36:53,940
for other international partners.

647
00:37:01,960 --> 00:37:04,960
The Apollo Soyuz program succeeded in bringing together

648
00:37:05,060 --> 00:37:07,360
Americans and Russians in space.

649
00:37:07,460 --> 00:37:10,360
It also ushered in a new era of other nations training

650
00:37:10,460 --> 00:37:12,360
at Star City.

651
00:37:12,361 --> 00:37:17,360
Ultimately, you had French astronauts and English scientists.

652
00:37:17,460 --> 00:37:21,360
There were apartment blocks built for crew members

653
00:37:21,460 --> 00:37:23,200
who would be visiting from other organizations

654
00:37:23,300 --> 00:37:24,300
and staying there.

655
00:37:24,400 --> 00:37:27,000
So it was a great time of expansion.

656
00:37:27,100 --> 00:37:30,120
Cosmonaut training had a new hurdle to overcome,

657
00:37:30,220 --> 00:37:33,820
how to build teams of cosmonauts from foreign countries.

658
00:37:33,920 --> 00:37:36,360
It had been hard enough among Russian crews.

659
00:37:36,460 --> 00:37:38,760
Now they had to do it among different cultures

660
00:37:38,860 --> 00:37:40,660
and nationalities.

661
00:37:42,360 --> 00:37:47,400
We had to forget we were individuals and concentrate on how to work as a team.

662
00:37:47,740 --> 00:37:52,160
We made ourselves see only what we had in common and not what separated us.

663
00:37:53,160 --> 00:37:56,100
For Americans, the training meant long hours.

664
00:37:56,560 --> 00:38:01,880
Before an astronaut acclimated to space, he or she had to get used to a new culture and system.

665
00:38:03,020 --> 00:38:04,540
The hours were fairly long.

666
00:38:04,541 --> 00:38:10,100
We would spend anywhere from four to six hours in Russian language class per day,

667
00:38:10,101 --> 00:38:14,100
another two to four hours in technical systems.

668
00:38:14,101 --> 00:38:16,100
You'd have to study in the evenings.

669
00:38:16,101 --> 00:38:18,100
It was a difficult day.

670
00:38:18,101 --> 00:38:22,100
Turning an astronaut into a cosmonaut was a challenge.

671
00:38:22,101 --> 00:38:25,100
The difficulties were compounded by the differences

672
00:38:25,101 --> 00:38:28,100
between the Soviet and American approach to training.

673
00:38:28,101 --> 00:38:31,100
American astronauts were subjected to all kinds of medical tests

674
00:38:31,101 --> 00:38:34,100
that they thought were unnecessary.

675
00:38:34,101 --> 00:38:36,100
Sometimes you had the doctors saying to an American astronaut,

676
00:38:36,101 --> 00:38:39,100
you will take this test, and an American astronaut saying,

677
00:38:39,101 --> 00:38:41,860
an astronaut saying, the hell I will.

678
00:38:41,861 --> 00:38:45,000
The basic difference that Star City

679
00:38:45,001 --> 00:38:48,960
has as a training facility, in contrast to NASA,

680
00:38:48,961 --> 00:38:53,340
is that it still has all the aspects

681
00:38:53,341 --> 00:38:56,340
of a military organization.

682
00:38:56,341 --> 00:38:58,420
Star City's strict military approach

683
00:38:58,421 --> 00:39:00,900
made cosmonaut training tougher on Americans,

684
00:39:00,901 --> 00:39:03,320
both on the ground and in space.

685
00:39:03,321 --> 00:39:06,400
American training stressed teaching astronauts to fly.

686
00:39:06,401 --> 00:39:09,100
But in cosmonaut training, they were required more often

687
00:39:09,101 --> 00:39:11,440
to ride.

688
00:39:11,540 --> 00:39:14,040
One American astronaut had what he thought

689
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:17,240
was a very cordial partnership with his Russian commander

690
00:39:17,340 --> 00:39:18,740
on the ground.

691
00:39:18,840 --> 00:39:22,040
When they got into space, it was entirely different.

692
00:39:22,140 --> 00:39:24,140
The Russian commander was the Russian commander,

693
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:25,540
and it was his way or the highway,

694
00:39:25,640 --> 00:39:27,560
and the highway was closed.

695
00:39:27,660 --> 00:39:30,260
It even extends to the point where a Russian commander will

696
00:39:30,360 --> 00:39:36,000
slap the hands of a passenger, whether he's an American or a

697
00:39:36,100 --> 00:39:38,060
French astronaut.

698
00:39:38,061 --> 00:39:40,900
By the start of cosmonaut training's third decade,

699
00:39:40,901 --> 00:39:44,740
Star City had prepared over 100 men and women for space,

700
00:39:44,741 --> 00:39:49,340
from 17 other countries, as well as the Soviet Union.

701
00:39:49,341 --> 00:39:51,400
What began as a program pioneering

702
00:39:51,401 --> 00:39:53,840
the unknown perils of space under the direction

703
00:39:53,841 --> 00:39:56,280
of physical trainers and doctors evolved

704
00:39:56,281 --> 00:39:59,240
into technical trainings in mock-ups and simulators.

705
00:39:59,241 --> 00:40:01,920
And finally, the complexities of training crews

706
00:40:01,921 --> 00:40:04,160
from different cultures and nationalities

707
00:40:04,161 --> 00:40:07,080
to work together for space expeditions measured

708
00:40:07,081 --> 00:40:07,980
in months at a time.

709
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:14,120
But of all the challenges it had to overcome,

710
00:40:14,121 --> 00:40:17,320
none was more difficult than the collapse of the Soviet Union

711
00:40:17,321 --> 00:40:20,960
in 1989.

712
00:40:20,961 --> 00:40:23,160
Russia's economic problems basically

713
00:40:23,161 --> 00:40:27,100
devastated Star City and all the technical institutes

714
00:40:27,101 --> 00:40:28,560
in the Soviet Union.

715
00:40:28,561 --> 00:40:29,740
There was no money.

716
00:40:29,741 --> 00:40:33,140
The ruble was devalued dozens of times.

717
00:40:33,141 --> 00:40:36,280
The management of Star City in the early 1990s

718
00:40:36,281 --> 00:40:38,440
and to this day has been forced to scramble,

719
00:40:38,540 --> 00:40:41,660
to take in tourists, train basically anybody

720
00:40:41,760 --> 00:40:44,220
who has a checkbook to pay the bills.

721
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:46,060
The Russian government was encouraged

722
00:40:46,160 --> 00:40:49,000
to make a deal to fly American astronauts

723
00:40:49,100 --> 00:40:51,520
in order to make money.

724
00:40:51,620 --> 00:40:55,560
The Russian economic crisis transformed Star City.

725
00:40:55,660 --> 00:40:58,560
Its vaunted training program became a financial asset

726
00:40:58,660 --> 00:41:00,440
to sell or rent.

727
00:41:00,540 --> 00:41:03,700
So was the Mir space station, the last great project

728
00:41:03,800 --> 00:41:05,480
of the Soviet Union.

729
00:41:05,481 --> 00:41:10,000
Out of necessity, Star City became partners with NASA's Johnson Space Center,

730
00:41:10,400 --> 00:41:12,440
training astronauts for visits to Mir.

731
00:41:13,840 --> 00:41:17,080
You could sign up for cosmonaut training yourself

732
00:41:17,081 --> 00:41:21,800
and spend $5,000 for a week or two of being tortured

733
00:41:21,801 --> 00:41:24,820
by former Soviet aerospace doctors and trainers.

734
00:41:25,440 --> 00:41:29,240
A 10-minute ride on the centrifuge sold for $1,000.

735
00:41:30,360 --> 00:41:33,460
And there were bigger and more expensive adventures for sale.

736
00:41:33,461 --> 00:41:41,180
An American millionaire named Dennis Tito paid $20 million to orbit the Earth with cosmonauts in 2001.

737
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:48,300
Through innovation and effort, Star City is surviving the new realities of the post-communist era.

738
00:41:49,040 --> 00:41:57,040
But the city, once famous as the first community dedicated to the exploration of outer space, may belong more to history than to the future.

739
00:41:57,041 --> 00:42:03,540
Cosmonauts still train here, but the pioneering efforts that invented space medicine, the

740
00:42:03,541 --> 00:42:09,040
isolation chamber, zero gravity tests, and the rigors of physical and mental conditioning

741
00:42:09,041 --> 00:42:10,040
are behind them.

742
00:42:10,041 --> 00:42:20,320
It evolved to a very effective level where crude as it might have been by Western standards,

743
00:42:20,321 --> 00:42:25,640
it still prepared people more than adequately for the stress of living in space.

744
00:42:25,641 --> 00:42:33,320
They have learned what it takes and do what needs to be done on these long-term visits

745
00:42:33,321 --> 00:42:37,660
where they're locked into chambers going around the Earth for six months at a time.

746
00:42:40,900 --> 00:42:42,060
We will continue.

747
00:42:42,420 --> 00:42:43,580
We will explore the stars.

748
00:42:43,960 --> 00:42:47,420
That's what we came to do, and we will find a way to do it at Star City.

749
00:42:47,421 --> 00:42:49,520
It is something that is in part of us.

750
00:42:50,600 --> 00:42:54,920
The future challenge of cosmonaut training will depend on what new mission

751
00:42:54,921 --> 00:42:57,360
Russian space planners come up with.

752
00:42:57,460 --> 00:43:00,620
If they are true to the earliest Russian space pioneers,

753
00:43:00,720 --> 00:43:03,120
it will be to colonize the planets.

754
00:43:03,220 --> 00:43:05,800
If that's the case, the next chapter

755
00:43:05,900 --> 00:43:09,260
may be to train whole families for space expeditions.

